  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "Walter Melville Wills was a British tobacco manufacturer from the Bristol business family. A Cambridge natural sciences graduate, he worked for Price Waterhouse before taking responsibility for the family firm’s finances. He became a partner and managing director of W. D. and H. O. Wills, later serving on Imperial Tobacco’s board.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 93,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "University history, local research and contemporary obituary agree on education and the professional sequence."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S2",
      "S3",
      "S4"
    ],
    "word_count": 51,
    "html": "<p>Walter Melville Wills was a British tobacco manufacturer from the Bristol business family. A Cambridge natural sciences graduate, he worked for Price Waterhouse before taking responsibility for the family firm’s finances. He became a partner and managing director of W. D. and H. O. Wills, later serving on Imperial Tobacco’s board.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Walter Melville Wills funded a new home for Bristol’s Homeopathic Hospital as a memorial to his son Bruce, who died in the First World War. He bought the site and commissioned Oatley and Lawrence to design the building. The hospital opened in 1925, with wards overlooking gardens and balconies for outdoor treatment.\n\nThe grounds received unusually close attention from Wills. He commissioned Pulham and Son to create terraces, pools and rockwork, and made clear that he would pay for an attractive garden. When the hospital committee raised the cost of maintaining the grounds, his family undertook to cover their upkeep.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 92,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "University chapter uses building-committee records; independent civic history corroborates the purpose and opening. Describes historical design, with no claim of medical efficacy."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S4",
      "S5"
    ],
    "word_count": 100,
    "html": "<p>Walter Melville Wills funded a new home for Bristol’s Homeopathic Hospital as a memorial to his son Bruce, who died in the First World War. He bought the site and commissioned Oatley and Lawrence to design the building. The hospital opened in 1925, with wards overlooking gardens and balconies for outdoor treatment.</p>\r\n<p>The grounds received unusually close attention from Wills. He commissioned Pulham and Son to create terraces, pools and rockwork, and made clear that he would pay for an attractive garden. When the hospital committee raised the cost of maintaining the grounds, his family undertook to cover their upkeep.</p>\r\n"
  },
